body:
Notes from Discord: the `span` macro does not allocate, which means that the overhead isn't `tracing` or `tracing-core`, but rather, `tracing-subscriber`. `tracing-subscriber`'s `Registry` and `Extensions` (which are used by `tracing-tracy`) to store profiling have room to improve performance, but a decent amount of the overhead you're experiencing comes from `tracing-subscriber::Registry` trying to be modular and flexible in accepting data for arbitrary user-defined Layers.

For a profiling use-case, it might be worthwhile to implement your own subscriber (replacing the `Registry` but using the underlying [`sharded-slab`](https://docs.rs/sharded-slab/latest/sharded_slab/) data structure without boxing values into extensions.)

@hawkw has some ideas to improve `tracing_subscriber::Registry`, where instead of using a `HashMap` for extensions, Layer types would be required to pre-declare extension types they'd be storing in the `Registry` and use arrays to store extensions. However, this will be a tracing-subscriber 0.4 change, which is not currently scheduled.

issue.body:
We've been extensively using `tracing_tracy` to profile and optimize [bevy](https://bevyengine.org). However, one potential pain point with our usage of the crate is the overhead incurred when entering a span. This has been reported in bevyengine/bevy#4892, where I'm seeing ~10us of overhead per span. Using these in tight loops artificially inflates the size of the surrounding span and is leading to inaccurate measurements. 

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3137680/173177048-86a526a8-8699-4ef4-ba8f-04afe71998f4.png)

@davidbarsky suggested that it might be a good idea to write a `tracing` subscriber instead of a Layer using `sharded_slab` and caching the allocation keys in the span instead of using extensions.
